[Film Theory Intro]

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:01.900
Hello Internet! Welcome to Film Theory!

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:07.300
Where today we're donning our mining hats and delving too greedily and too deep into the theory mines.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:11.500
In order to unearth the answer to the biggest question of the Lord of the Rings

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.500
- no, not why the Hobbit movies were so bad, not even I have the answers to everything.

00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:20.000
No, today I am delivering the definitive answer to the Internet's favorite Lord of the Rings theory -

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.500
"Why didn't Frodo not just ride the Eagles to Mount Doom and drop the Ring in?"

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.700
We all know they exist, we know they can pretty much go anywhere,

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:32.200
we know they're friendly to the ringbearer's side, so why the heck did team Ring just ride up to Mordor?

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.549
Elrond: "Morrrdorrr"

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:35.200
*Elrond saying Mordor like a boss*

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:38.500
Sorry, Morrrrdorrrrrr - in the first place.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:41.400
Honestly, it's like the movie is begging us to ask this question.

00:01:41.470 --> 00:01:47.429
85 minutes and 48 seconds into the very first movie - if you're watching the extended edition  -

00:01:47.429 --> 00:01:50.100
Which, is there any other way to watch these movies?

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:55.200
We see Gandalf GTF out of Isengard on the back of a giant Eagle after sending them off to go get it for him.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.500
Like some sort of high fantasy Uber.

00:01:57.569 --> 00:02:00.800
Why else introduce the perfect solution to every problem so early?

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:05.800
Just hit up your Moth app call an Eagle and we got this whole trilogy wrapped up in a cool 45

00:02:05.849 --> 00:02:09.800
Well, according to one of the most famous and most popular fan theories of all time

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:15.400
This wasn't a plot hole at all. The plan was to use the Eagles to fly to Mordor all along.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:21.500
It's a great theory, really, and if Film Theory existed back in 2001, I'd be all over this one like a horde of orcs on...

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:24.840
this other ork that they just decided to randomly cannibalize.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:30.040
Coined the "fly you fools" theory was made popular by an infographic from the website tickld,

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.200
which miraculously isn't a porn site, go figure

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.600
The image spread quickly and the full theory was proposed by reddit user VulcanDeathGrip

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.200
which, you know, clap and a half right out of the gate for the amazing user name.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.600
To sum it up, here's the theory.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:48.300
Our hero wizard Gandalf is imprisoned by the evil wizard Saruman for failing to join his evil master plan.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:54.000
Gandalf orders up a giant Eagle lift on his smart moth and boom, giant Eagle arrives and zooms Gandalf away

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.200
But instead of heading straight to Rivendell to meet Frodo and the rest of the Fellowship,

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:00.500
the theory says that Gandalf instead flies to the Eagles Eyrie,

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:05.000
which is a real canonical thing established in Middle-earth to talk with the king of the Eagles.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.800
The Eyrie is actually pretty close to Rivendell, but it's on the eastern side of the Misty Mountains.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:17.000
A detail that's important because when Gandalf is escaping on the Eagle, we actually see him veer east over the mountains

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.800
So, the directions with this theory actually match up.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:25.200
Once there, Gandalf, according to the theory, hatches a plan to use the Eagles to fly the Ring to Mordor,

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:29.000
something we never see or hear any word of in the books or movies.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.300
Gandalf and the Eagles decide to keep this plan a secret because they need to retain the element of surprise,

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:37.000
even retaining that surprise from all the readers and viewers apparently...

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.200
So now the question becomes where to meet up with the Eagles for maximum secrecy.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.600
Gandalf decides they should meet up in the South because canonically,

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:46.300
all the other areas are overrun with either orcs or agents of Sauron.

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.600
And they have to meet on this side of the Misty Mountains because again, according to the theory,

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.900
the weather in the mountains is too dangerous for the Eagles to fly over.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.500
That might seem like a bit of a stretch,

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:59.400
but the story does happen in winter and the mountains in the Lord of the Rings were modeled after

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:02.900
the European Alps, where it's literally like minus 46 degrees in winter,

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:05.100
so it's plausible to say the least.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:08.900
The only other option for the trip would have been to avoid the Misty Mountains entirely

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:11.340
and go all the way south to the gap of Rohan.

00:04:11.400 --> 00:04:15.900
But, as literally everyone tells Boromir every time he brings it up,

00:04:15.900 --> 00:04:19.800
this is right next to Isengard aka Saruman's base of operations,

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:23.400
meaning all the Eagles would get spotted immediately and the plan is busted.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:28.590
So, left with no other options, they decide to got to travel on foot to the other side of the mountains, meet the Eagles

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.600
and then finally fly to Mordor in the south

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.900
Unfortunately, the group can't make it over the mountains due to, you know, that aforementioned nasty weather,

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.800
and the Fellowship decides to go for

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.500
Boromir: "Make for the Gap of Rohan"

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.180
No, shut up, Boromir, stop suggesting it!

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:44.920
They go through "the mines of Moria"

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:48.000
Where Pippin literally gets Gandalf killed because he is the worst,

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:52.200
but right before Gandalf falls he utters these three fateful words

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.600
Gandalf: "Fly, you fools"

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:59.400
According to the theory, this was Gandalf telling the Fellowship what they were supposed to do - fly using the Eagles

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:01.900
But, go figure, team Ring is too thick to get it.

00:05:01.900 --> 00:05:03.420
Honestly though, who can blame them?

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:08.600
Instead of using two of his last three remaining words to insult his team Gandalf could have made things a bit more explicit

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:13.300
Like, instead of "fly, you fools" he could have said "fly to Mordor", "fly using Eagles",

00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.600
even something as simple as "fly like an ea..."

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.500
I'm sure everyone could have figured out the rest. *SPOILER: Fly Like an Eagle by Steve Miller*

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:21.400
So anyway, that's the theory and honestly I think it's a really cool theory.

00:05:21.400 --> 00:05:23.400
The problem is: it's just wrong.

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.000
You take it from me, I know a thing or two about wrong theories.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:29.200
That's right, for once in my life I'm giving myself an early Christmas present

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.400
and I get to poke holes into someone else's theory!

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.300
Wow, how cathartic to turn the tables for a change!

00:05:35.300 --> 00:05:39.000
Today I'm gonna shoot down the Eagle Theory, pun definitely intended,

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:43.100
because it is literally the worst strategy for the Fellowship ever!

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:47.300
I mean honestly, it's almost worse than just straight-up giving the Ring to Sauron.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:51.500
In doing this theory, I didn't just rely on old reddit posts from 2012, oh no.

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:52.400
I went digging.

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:58.600
I busted out the Lord of the Rings books, I got the Silmarillion, I pored over the letters Tolkien wrote to pretty much everybody.

00:05:58.600 --> 00:06:05.300
I picked up the Unfinished Tales, the collection of books that are compiled by Christopher Tolkien, J.R.R.'s editor, map creator and publisher.

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:08.500
Books filled with partial stories and scraps of lore.

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:13.600
I spent days poring over tens of thousands of pages and not e-book pages mind you,

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:18.700
literal physical actual paper pages because Christopher Tolkien is some sort of technophobe

00:06:18.700 --> 00:06:22.100
that won't allow the more obscure nerdy titles to be published in an electronic format.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:26.900
So out the window with my CTRL+F function and in the window with my analog CTRL+F function

00:06:26.900 --> 00:06:30.500
which is just an index and let me just say it those things sucked

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:34.600
One more disclaimer here, because I know that some people don't consider the Unfinished Tales and letters

00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:37.100
to be canon, because it's that kind of fanbase,

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:42.000
so I only went with those sources insofar as they didn't contradict anything in the actual books.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.200
If there was ever a disagreement, I always defaulted to the information that was actually published.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:48.600
And what did I learn from doing all that? Well...

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:53.400
That the lore of Middle-earth is really long and sometimes really dry

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:55.800
And kinda glosses over some big things.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:00.900
Like the destruction of an entire continent that gets explained away in a whopping one paragraph

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:05.800
So, the first thing to know is that in Tolkien's lore most of the stuff in Middle-earth was created by three gods

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:11.240
Monwë, Yavanna, and Aulë. Yes, Middle-earth has gods, gods that, let's be honest,

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:16.000
were probably a bit pissed that in three Hobbit movies scraping the absolute bottom of the barrel

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.700
for any content to fill their excessive runtimes, they still didn't make the cut. Anyway,

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.560
*Dramatic deep intro voice* In the beginning

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.200
Yavanna created all the plants, trees, forests and animals.

00:07:26.200 --> 00:07:28.100
Aulë comes in and makes the Dwarves.

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:32.300
Yavana, worried that the Dwarves would literally just mow down the forests to make stuff,

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:34.300
creates the ents to protect the trees.

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:37.700
Monwë, feeling a bit left out, sees the stuff the other two are creating and goes:

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:42.000
MatPat as Monwë: "Ah sweet, would you help me make some Eagles? Like, big ones! Big Eagles!"

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:47.400
And boom! Yavanna and Monwë made the Eagles and thus the Eagles came into existence

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:48.100
*More serene intro voice* The end

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.300
Now, why would you care about any of this stuff?

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:54.900
Well, it shows us that the Eagles go super far back in the history of this world

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:58.600
And, in general, the older stuff is in Middle-earth, the more powerful it is.

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:00.500
Except for the Dwarves. Sorry, Gimli.

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:04.400
But that means, in the hierarchy of Middle-earth, Eagles rank way up there.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:07.500
The gods, or Valar as they're called, are at the top.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:12.300
Directly below them are the demigods, or Maiar. This tier has some familiar faces

00:08:12.380 --> 00:08:14.960
Sauron, Saruman, and Gandalf.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:19.800
And the Eagles are just slightly below them. In fact, Tolkien himself was a bit wishy-washy on this point,

00:08:19.800 --> 00:08:22.800
saying that, hey the Eagles are pretty much unofficially Maiar,

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.700
but anyway, suffice it to say they rank above all the other races

00:08:25.700 --> 00:08:27.140
So, why does any of this matter?

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:31.600
Well canonically, and this is a point that the movies don't really do a good job of explaining,

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:35.600
the Ring of Power works proportionally to the power of the holder.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:38.700
You ever wonder why a hobbit was really the safest choice to be ring bearer?

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:43.360
Or why this all-powerful ring that everyone is so scared of only manages to turn people invisible?

00:08:43.370 --> 00:08:47.500
It's because Hobbits are a really low power race in this world and because of that

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:53.500
the Ring of Power is the least dangerous in their hands. The One Ring can only really use what's the in the wearer.

00:08:53.500 --> 00:08:58.000
Which isn't that much when it's being carried by a Hobbit. Now, contrast that to the giant Eagles who are

00:08:58.079 --> 00:09:03.200
practically demigods and who, according to Tolkien himself, have a wingspan of 30 fathoms. *wow!*

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:06.400
Putting them at half the size of a football field *wow, again*

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:11.900
And they're not just big. During the War of Wrath, which was a huge war before the Lord of the Rings series happened,

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:19.200
they were able to kill the dragon that was so enormous that when he crash-landed he collapsed three giant active volcanoes.

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:25.800
These Eagles are massive, powerful, and they're immortals who are more powerful than all the other races on Middle-earth

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:30.400
Oh, yeah, and they can also canonically see through walls. The Eagles are more than just mere birds.

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:35.300
They are no joke and they definitely don't need their power amped up by the One Ring.

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:39.200
If you give the Eagles an opportunity to carry the One Ring you're looking at potentially

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:42.400
the most powerful Dark Lords in the entire series.

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.000
If they can manage to fit the ring on their talons that is.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.000
If you think about it, this is the same reason why the Ring has to stay out of everyone else's hands.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.800
This is why Gandalf never directly touches the Ring and tries to keep it out of his sight as much as possible.

00:09:54.800 --> 00:09:57.500
Even telling Frodo that through him it would

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:00.700
Gandalf: "wield a power too Great and terrible to imagine"

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:02.700
Because Gandalf is a Mayar demigod.

00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:06.800
The classic Galadriel freakout scene is her basically predicting her own power amped up on the Ring,

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:11.500
which is again why so many people are required to protect the darn thing.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.700
MatPat: "Hey, hey, Boromir, my eyes are up here. Hey!"

00:10:14.700 --> 00:10:20.300
Also notice when the Eagles do come into play: to rescue Sam and Frodo from Mount Doom as it collapses

00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:23.000
When the Ring of Power is no longer around.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:29.200
As soon as it's destroyed and out of the running, then it's safe to bring the Eagles in, because they're no longer gonna be tempted

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:33.900
No one's at risk and that's exactly when Gandalf calls for their help again

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:36.800
But there's more to this plan being stupid than just raw power

00:10:36.889 --> 00:10:41.300
Sauron literally doesn't comprehend the possibility that anyone with the Ring would want to destroy it.

00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:45.900
And he doesn't discover this plan until mere moments before the Ring is tossed into the heat of Mount Doom.

00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:49.700
He can't defend himself if he doesn't know what he's defending against.

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:53.700
That's why it's imperative that the Fellowship remain a secret from everyone.

00:10:53.700 --> 00:10:58.000
And the Eagles? Remember, they're the size of Boeing 747s up there

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:02.820
Trying to conduct a stealth mission with a giant Eagle to drop the ring into the pits of Mount Doom would be

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:06.100
like trying to sneak into a Jamba Juice with a fighter jet.

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:08.800
Or, you know what, maybe we should just believe Tolkien himself.

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:14.700
In a letter from June 1958 where he criticizes an unofficial Lord of the Rings film treatmen sent to him

00:11:14.700 --> 00:11:17.420
going so far as to say it's murdered his story.

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:22.200
Yeah, that's harsh feedback. Tolkien directly comments on the script's overuse of the Eagles.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:28.600
Quote: "The Eagles are a dangerous 'machine'. I've used them sparingly and that is the absolute limit of their credibility"

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:33.700
End quote. Using the Eagles to solve all your problems just doesn't make for a good story

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:35.900
And that's ultimately what we as an audience are here for.

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:40.000
And Tolkien, being the incredible writer that he is, knew just that.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:45.280
But hey! That's just a theory! A Film Theory!

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:46.400
Aaaaaaaand

00:11:46.400 --> 00:11:47.300
cut!

00:11:47.300 --> 00:11:53.240
Man, finding loopholes in theories is fun and cathartic. Maybe I should just chang this channel to be The Theory Buster

